---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProgressDrawSchedule from '@/components/draws/ProgressDrawSchedule';
import '@/components/draws/draws.css';

const path = '/progress-draw-schedule/';
const title = 'Progress Draw Schedule & Retainage Calculator | Invoice Workshop';
const description =
  'Free progress draw calculator on the AIA G702/G703 model: schedule of values, work completed, stored materials, retainage per line and the payment due this period.';
const software = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Progress Draw Schedule & Retainage Calculator',
  applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication', operatingSystem: 'Web',
  url: new URL(path, Astro.site).toString(), description,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  featureList: 'Schedule of values, per-line retainage, percent complete, payment due, browser-only',
};
const breadcrumb = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Invoice Workshop', item: 'https://invoiceworkshop.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Progress Draw Schedule', item: new URL(path, Astro.site).toString() },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={[software, breadcrumb]}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Progress billing without the spreadsheet</p>
    <h1>Progress Draw Schedule &amp; Retainage Calculator</h1>
    <p class="hero-intro">
      Build a schedule of values, enter what was completed previously and this period,
      and get the retainage and the payment due on the same column model an architect or
      lender reviews against. Free, no signup, and nothing leaves your browser.
    </p>
    <p class="privacy-reassurance">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5.5 8V6a4.5 4.5 0 0 1 9 0v2M4 8h12v9H4z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"/></svg>
      Your schedule is saved in this browser only. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </section>
  <ProgressDrawSchedule client:load />
  <div class="content-shell">
    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The columns, and why they are lettered</h2>
      <div class="content-copy">
        <p>
          A pay application is not reviewed as a total. It is reviewed column by column
          against the schedule of values agreed at the start of the job, which is why the
          AIA G703 continuation sheet letters them. A schedule that invents its own columns
          is one the reviewer cannot check line against line, and that is what gets a draw
          held.
        </p>
        <dl class="term-list">
          <dt>C — Scheduled value</dt><dd>What the line is worth in the contract. These add to the contract sum.</dd>
          <dt>D — Completed in previous applications</dt><dd>Work already billed and certified before this period.</dd>
          <dt>E — Completed this period</dt><dd>Work put in place since the last application.</dd>
          <dt>F — Materials presently stored</dt><dd>Delivered to site and paid for, not yet installed. Billable on most contracts, and the column most often disputed.</dd>
          <dt>G — Total completed and stored to date</dt><dd>D + E + F. This is the figure percent complete and retainage are both taken from.</dd>
          <dt>I — Balance to finish</dt><dd>C − G. A negative number here means the line is over-billed, which this tool shows rather than hides.</dd>
          <dt>J — Retainage</dt><dd>The percentage withheld from completed work as security until substantial completion. Typically 5% or 10%.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Where the arithmetic goes wrong</h2>
      <div class="content-copy">
        <p>
          Two mistakes account for most disputed draws, and both are arithmetic rather than
          judgement.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first is taking retainage on the total instead of summing the column. Round
          each line and add them, and the figure differs from a percentage of the total by a
          few cents on a long schedule. That difference is small and it is also the one a
          reviewer finds first, because the column visibly does not add to the box beneath
          it. This tool sums the column.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second is applying a retainage reduction line by line. Many contracts halve
          retainage once the job passes 50% complete — the job, not a line. Releasing
          retainage on a finished line while the project is a quarter built bills money the
          contract does not yet owe, and it is the kind of error that gets an entire
          application returned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>A worked draw</h2>
      <div class="content-copy">
        <p>
          A $200,000 contract, 10% retainage, with $60,000 billed previously and $40,000
          this period.
        </p>
        <figure class="doc-table-wrap">
          <figcaption>Application 2 — total completed $100,000 against a $200,000 contract</figcaption>
          <div class="doc-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Application 2 payment rollup">
            <table class="doc-table">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col" class="numeric">Amount</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Contract sum</th><td class="numeric">$200,000.00</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Total completed and stored to date</th><td class="numeric">$100,000.00</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Less retainage at 10%</th><td class="numeric">−$10,000.00</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Less previous certificates</th><td class="numeric">−$54,000.00</td></tr>
              </tbody>
              <tfoot>
                <tr><th scope="row">Current payment due</th><td class="numeric">$36,000.00</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Balance to finish, including retainage</th><td class="numeric">$110,000.00</td></tr>
              </tfoot>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="doc-table-note">
            The previous certificate is $54,000, not $60,000: the earlier application was
            also net of its own 10% retainage. Subtracting the gross figure understates the
            payment due by $6,000, and it is the single most common error on a second draw.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <div class="content-copy">
        <p>
          This calculates a draw. It is not an AIA document, is not a substitute for the
          G702 and G703 forms where a contract requires those specific forms, and does not
          carry an architect's certification. Retainage limits, and whether retainage may be
          held on stored materials at all, are set by contract and by state law and differ
          considerably. Check your contract.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="related-tools content-section">
      <div class="related-heading"><h2>Related business tools</h2><p>Bill the draw once the schedule is agreed.</p></div>
      <div class="related-grid">
        <a class="related-card" href="/construction-invoice-template/"><strong>Construction Invoice Template</strong><span>Itemize labor, materials and deposits on a construction job.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/contractor-invoice-template/"><strong>Contractor Invoice Template</strong><span>Project and deposit fields for contract work.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/work-order-generator/"><strong>Work Order Generator</strong><span>Document authorized work before it is billed.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/receipt-generator/"><strong>Receipt Generator</strong><span>Confirm the draw payment when it arrives.</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
